@title Rotating TLS certificates on the edge proxies
@subtitle Redwater Media Platform runbook, owned by Platform Security
@author Platform Security, Owner

kpi Rotate at | 30 days or fewer left | CERT_EXPIRY_30D | neutral
kpi Edge proxies | 18 | 1 canary + 17 | neutral
kpi Hostnames | 12 | covered by the certificates | neutral
kpi Page Platform Security | > 2% | handshake errors on any proxy | bad

## When to rotate

Rotate when a certificate has 30 days or fewer left. The `CERT_EXPIRY_30D` alert announces it. The certificates cover 12 hostnames across the 18 edge proxies.

## Before you start

- Have the change record open.
- Confirm no deploy is running in #deploys.
- Check that the internal CA is healthy.

## Rotation steps

1. Confirm expiry
-- Run `openssl x509 -enddate -noout` on the live certificate.
-- Compare the date with the alert.
2. Request the new certificate
-- Run `certctl request --profile edge-prod`.
-- Check that the output lists all 12 hostnames.
3. Stage it on the canary proxy `edge-canary-01` only
-- Copy it to `/etc/edge/certs/next`.
-- Reload the proxy.
4. Verify the canary
-- `openssl s_client -connect edge-canary-01:443` shows the new expiry date.
-- Handshake errors stay under 0.1% for 20 minutes.
5. Roll out to the other 17 proxies in batches of 3, waiting 5 minutes between batches.
6. Revoke the old certificate after 48 hours and record it in the change record.

!! Never delete the old private key before step 6. You need it for rollback until the old certificate is revoked.

## Rollback

The previous certificate stays in `/etc/edge/certs/previous` for 7 days. Copy it back and reload the proxy.

## Escalation

- Page Platform Security if any proxy shows more than 2% handshake errors.
- Post in #platform-sec.
